{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lgunh3xdo2e48ZAu4wvqRU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3dge, lean\n2\t\n3\tThe my-3dge game engine as a library, the way three.js or Rapier ship: one ES module, nothing to install, build or\n4\ttest. It makes 3D games for the browser: three.js r182 draws on WebGPU, Rapier simulates the physics, a fixed 60 Hz\n5\tsim runs the rules, and HTML/CSS draws the UI. This file is all you need, in a chat with no sandbox too: a game is\n6\tone HTML file.\n7\t\n8\t## Start\n9\t\n10\t1. Take `game.html` (in full below): a small game that already plays. You run round an arena and collect the gems\n11\t   before the clock runs out.\n12\t2. Change it into the game you are asked for. Keep its head: it loads the engine, three.js and Rapier from jsDelivr.\n13\t3. Open it in a browser with WebGPU (current Chrome, Edge or Safari; Firefox where it has WebGPU): double-click it, or\n14\t   serve its folder (`npx serve`, `python3 -m http.server`). There is no WebGL fallback.\n15\t\n16\tA bigger game can split its code into module files that each import `my3dge`; serve the folder then, since browsers\n17\tload module files over http, not from a double-clicked page.\n18\t\n19\t## How a game is made\n20\t\n21\t**Two halves.** The _sim_ is the rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them every\n22\tstep, and events. The _page_ calls `createEngine`, which starts the sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into\n23\tintents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame` for the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The\n24\tpage reads the sim and never writes it.\n25\t\n26\t**A scene** is a game mode: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings, setup(w), step(w, intents) })`.\n27\t`setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. A page offers its scenes with\n28\t`createEngine({ scenes })`, and `?scene=<id>` picks one.\n29\t\n30\t**The world, `w`,** is what systems get:\n31\t\n32\t- `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields (`number`, `integer`,\n33\t  `string`, `boolean`) with defaults. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an id; `w.query('gem')` returns the\n34\t  entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)` the entity (`.gem`), `w.add(id, 'gem', values)`, `w.remove(id,\n35\t  'gem')` and `w.despawn(id)` do the rest. A component with `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box, which\n36\t  also reads `y` (its bottom), `yaw`, `size` (its side, 0.5 m) and `material` (an id): name a place you only keep\n37\t  otherwise.\n38\t- Systems run in phases, in order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`:\n39\t  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` runs after the physics moved the bodies.\n40\t- `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)`; a page reads\n41\t  `frame.world.events.trace()`.\n42\t- Time is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800).\n43\t- Randomness comes from named streams: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n44\t- Settings are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum, when:\n45\t  'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`, set from the URL (`?game.gems=20`).\n46\t\n47\t**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once a step: `move` (a direction `[x, z]`), buttons held and\n48\tpressed, and the game's own keys. A player actor (`spawnActor(w, { player: true, … })`) reads them itself; systems\n49\tget them as their second argument (`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). WASD or the arrows run,\n50\tSpace jumps, Shift dashes.\n51\t\n52\t**The HUD** is the page's: `engine.ui.hud(id, text, { at })` sets a line at one of six places (`top-left`, `top`,\n53\t`top-right`, `bottom-left`, `bottom`, `bottom-right`), and `null` as its text removes it.\n54\t\n55\t**Particles, sound and lights.** The sim says what happened with events; the page turns them into particles and\n56\tsound, so play stays reproducible:\n57\t\n58\t- A sim event bursts every emitter whose `events` names its type, where its payload says (`position`, or `x`, `y`,\n59\t  `z`): `w.emit('hit', { x, y: 1, z })` throws sparks. To burst others on your own event, lay them into an emitter:\n60\t  `defineEmitter('emitter:lit', { events: ['lit'], layers: [{ emitter: 'emitter:fireflies', count: 24 }] })`, then\n61\t  `w.emit('lit', { x, y: 1.2, z })`.\n62\t- Sound plays in the page: `const audio = createAudio();` once (it starts at the player's first key or click), then\n63\t  `audio.play('sfx:coin')`, or in `onFrame` each new event whose payload names one, `w.emit('lit', { sfx:\n64\t  'sfx:powerup', x, z })` in the sim: `for (const e of frame.world.events.trace()) if (e.seq > heard) { heard =\n65\t  e.seq; audio.event(e.type, e.payload); }`.\n66\t- A prop with a light (`prop:lantern`, `prop:lamp`, …) is switchable when spawned with `{ on }` or `{ group }`:\n67\t  `const id = spawnProp(w, { prop: 'prop:lantern', at: [x, 0, z], yaw: 0 }, { on: false })`, then\n68\t  `w.get(id).propLight.on = true` lights it, and `switchGroup(w, group, true)` a group.\n69\t\n70\t**Characters, physics and the rest** are in the API below, area by area, and every built-in id under [Built-in ids\n71\tand the level legend](#built-in-ids-and-the-level-legend): levels as text (`compileLevel`: a tile at column c and\n72\trow r is at x = (c + 0.5) × tile, z = (r + 0.5) × tile; `level.spawns` are `{ name, position }`, `'hero'` and `'1'`\n73\tto `'9'`) and sites of buildings on a terrain (`defineSite`),\n74\tcharacters (`createCharacter`, Apex Legends' movement with `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`: sprint, slide, mantle,\n75\tclimb; `spawnActor`), physics (`physicsOf(w)` and its `.raycast`, `.overlap`), props, ladders, ziplines, doors,\n76\twindows and switches, crowds and steering, combat, melee and firearms (`addMelee`, `defineWeapon`), materials and\n77\ttextures as data, looks and post-processing, particle emitters, sound as data, cameras (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=chase`,\n78\tthe shooter's `?cam3=shoulder`), the HUD (`engine.ui.hud`), menus and a store for saves and settings (`createMenu`,\n79\t`createStore`, `createEngine({ store, modal })`) and touch controls.\n80\t\n81\t## Rules that keep play reproducible\n82\t\n83\tThe engine runs the same seed and the same intents to the same state, bit for bit, on every machine. Keep these in\n84\tthe sim (a scene's `setup`, `step` and systems) and replays, `__engine.hash()` and capture and restore keep working:\n85\t\n86\t- No `Date`, timers, `Math.random`, DOM, network or storage in the sim. Randomness from `w.rng`, time in steps.\n87\t- Write the standard `Math` (the sim swaps in portable versions while it steps), but never keep a `Math` function in a\n88\t  variable, and write `Math.pow`, not `**`.\n89\t- Keep the sim's state in components, not in variables beside it; loop over entities in `w.query`'s order.\n90\t- Presentation never writes the sim: the camera, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change nothing a hash sees.\n91\t- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n92\t\n93\t## Make it look different\n94\t\n95\tHow a game looks is data, apart from how it plays: a _look_ is a material style, a chain of filters and a sky. The\n96\tsame game can be a storybook, a film or pixel art with nothing else changed, and no look changes a replay or a hash.\n97\t\n98\t- **Pick a look:** `?post.look=look:storybook` in the URL. To make it the game's own, set it where game.html sets its\n99\t  camera: `if (!search.has('post.look')) search.set('post.look', 'look:storybook');`. Live, from the console:\n100\t  `__engine.set('post.look', 'look:cinematic')`.\n101\t- **Lay looks on and tune them:** `post.combine` lays looks over the one drawn, each `{ look, amount, only, filters }`\n102\t  (`amount` 0 to 1, `only` some of its filters, `filters` changes to them); `post.filters` changes filters field by\n103\t  field; `post.strength` (0 to 1) fades the whole result. In a URL their values are JSON:\n104\t  `?post.combine=[{\"look\":\"look:vignette/soft\",\"amount\":0.6}]&post.filters={\"grade\":{\"scope\":\"all\",\"saturation\":1.3}}`.\n105\t- **Where a filter applies:** its `scope`: `'all'`, `'characters'` (the actors' and crowds' bodies), `'objects'`\n106\t  (props), `'environment'` (the rest: the level, the terrain, stand-in boxes, the sky), two joined by `+`\n107\t  (`'characters+objects'`), or `'off'`. Every filter has an `amount`, 0 (as if it were off) to 1.\n108\t- **Make your own:** `defineLook` before `createEngine`, then pick it like any look:\n109\t\n110\t  ```js\n111\t  defineLook('look:warm-tale', {\n112\t    description: 'A storybook with soft corners and inked heroes.',\n113\t    combine: [{ look: 'look:storybook' }, { look: 'look:vignette/soft', amount: 0.6 }],\n114\t    filters: { outline: { scope: 'characters', width: 2 }, grade: { scope: 'all', temperature: 0.2 } },\n115\t  });\n116\t  ```\n117\t\n118\t- **Read it back:** `__engine.look()` gives the look drawn now: its id, the looks laid on, the strength, and every\n119\t  filter that is on with its scope, amount and numbers; its `filters` pasted into a `defineLook` keeps the picture.\n120\t\n121\tHow they combine: a look's `combine` goes on first, layer by layer, then its own `filters`, each of which replaces a\n122\tlayer's filter of its name whole, its unwritten fields at their defaults (so `look:warm-tale` inks only the\n123\tcharacters, though `look:storybook` inks everything). A layer at an `amount` below 1 fades in a filter the chain lacks\n124\t(its `amount` scaled) and moves one the chain has that far: numbers move, colours mix, and choices (a scope, a kind)\n125\tswitch from 0.5. A filter stays off\n126\tuntil something gives it a scope: `grade: { temperature: 0.2 }` alone does nothing. A layer never turns a filter off,\n127\tnor does `scope: 'off'` in a look's own `filters`: leave a layer's filter out with `only`, or set its scope to `'off'`\n128\tin that layer's `filters`; `post.filters`, applied last, turns one off outright. Only filters are laid on: the style,\n129\tthe sky and the rest come from the look `post.look` names. Changing a filter's numbers, scope or amount, or the looks\n130\tlaid on, builds no shader. Every look, what it draws, and every filter are under [Looks and\n131\tfilters](#looks-and-filters), below game.html.\n132\t\n133\t## Look inside a running game\n134\t\n135\tThe browser console has `__engine`: `__engine.help()` lists it all; `step(n)`, `hash()`, `state('round')`,\n136\t`set('game.gems', 20)`, `seed(7)`, `camera.set('cam=iso')`, `look()`, and `describe('material')`, a kind's fields and\n137\tids. The URL takes the same: `?seed=7&game.gems=20&cam=iso`.\n138\tErrors name their fix, and docs/ERRORS.md in the engine's repository lists every code.\n139\t\n140\t## Versions\n141\t\n142\tThe head's URLs pin three.js and Rapier, and take the engine at `@main`, its latest: jsDelivr serves a new commit\n143\twithin 12 hours, and a browser may keep the copy it loaded for up to 7 days (jsDelivr's cache header). To freeze a\n144\tgame on one engine, replace `main` with a commit of https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge in its two engine URLs\n145\t(`my3dge.js`, `my3dge.css`).\n146\t\n147\tMore: the engine's repository has the full guide (docs/GAME-GUIDE.md), every export (docs/API.md) and the source. An\n148\tagent that can run commands and wants the engine's checks too can start from the repository's README instead.\n149\tLicense: MIT; `my3dge.js` carries @stdlib functions (Apache-2.0): see LICENSE beside this file.\n150\t\n151\t## game.html\n152\t\n153\t<!-- lean:game:start -->\n154\t```html\n155\t<!doctype html>\n156\t<html lang=\"en\">\n157\t  <head>\n158\t    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n159\t    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no\" />\n160\t    <title>Gem run: a my-3dge game</title>\n161\t    <!--\n162\t      A whole game in one page: run round an arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out. Make your game by\n163\t      changing this file (lean/README.md says how). Open it in a browser with WebGPU: double-click it, or serve its\n164\t      folder. The block below loads the engine, three.js and Rapier from jsDelivr; keep it as it is.\n165\t    -->\n166\t    <!-- lean:head:start -->\n167\t    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/michaelcrosato/my-3dge@main/lean/my3dge.css\" />\n168\t    <script type=\"importmap\">\n169\t      {\n170\t        \"imports\": {\n171\t          \"my3dge\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/michaelcrosato/my-3dge@main/lean/my3dge.js\",\n172\t          \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.js\",\n173\t          \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.js\",\n174\t          \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.js\",\n175\t          \"three/addons/\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/examples/jsm/\",\n176\t          \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.21.0/dist/rapier.mjs\"\n177\t        }\n178\t      }\n179\t    </script>\n180\t    <!-- lean:head:end -->\n181\t    <style>\n182\t      html,\n183\t      body {\n184\t        margin: 0;\n185\t        height: 100%;\n186\t        overflow: hidden;\n187\t        background: #15171c;\n188\t      }\n189\t      canvas {\n190\t        display: block;\n191\t        width: 100vw;\n192\t        height: 100dvh;\n193\t        outline: none;\n194\t        touch-action: none;\n195\t      }\n196\t      #msg {\n197\t        position: fixed;\n198\t        inset: 0;\n199\t        display: flex;\n200\t        align-items: center;\n201\t        justify-content: center;\n202\t        padding: 24px;\n203\t        font:\n204\t          18px system-ui,\n205\t          sans-serif;\n206\t        color: #c9ccd4;\n207\t        text-align: center;\n208\t        white-space: pre-line;\n209\t        pointer-events: none;\n210\t      }\n211\t    </style>\n212\t    <script>\n213\t      // Until the engine starts, a script that fails shows here in one line, never as a blank page.\n214\t      window.__engine = { ready: false, errors: [] };\n215\t      function failed(message) {\n216\t        if (typeof window.__engine.help === 'function') return; // the engine shows its own errors\n217\t        window.__engine.errors.push({\n218\t          code: 'APP_START',\n219\t          message: message,\n220\t          fix: 'the browser console names the cause',\n221\t        });\n222\t        document.getElementById('msg').textContent = 'The game failed to start: ' + message;\n223\t      }\n224\t      addEventListener('error', (event) => failed(event.message || 'a script failed to load'));\n225\t      addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (event) => failed(String(event.reason?.message ?? event.reason)));\n226\t    </script>\n227\t  </head>\n228\t  <body>\n229\t    <canvas id=\"view\"></canvas>\n230\t    <div id=\"msg\" role=\"status\">Loading…</div>\n231\t    <script type=\"module\">\n232\t      import {\n233\t        addLevelBodies,\n234\t        compileLevel,\n235\t        createCharacter,\n236\t        createEngine,\n237\t        defineComponent,\n238\t        defineScene,\n239\t        defineSettings,\n240\t        physicsOf,\n241\t        spawnActor,\n242\t      } from 'my3dge';\n243\t\n244\t      // ---- The sim: everything that decides play. It runs 60 steps a second, the same on every machine. ----\n245\t\n246\t      // The arena as text: # wall, . floor, P pillar, w low wall (jump it), @ the player's spawn. 1 m a character.\n247\t      const ARENA_TEXT = `---\n248\tdescription: The arena. 18 × 14 m inside walls, two pillars, two low walls to jump, the player's spawn.\n249\t---\n250\t##################\n251\t#................#\n252\t#..@.............#\n253\t#.......PP.......#\n254\t#.......PP.......#\n255\t#................#\n256\t#....w......w....#\n257\t#....w......w....#\n258\t#................#\n259\t#.......PP.......#\n260\t#.......PP.......#\n261\t#................#\n262\t#................#\n263\t##################\n264\t`;\n265\t      const ARENA = compileLevel(ARENA_TEXT, { file: 'game.html', name: 'arena' });\n266\t\n267\t      // Settings: change them from the URL (?game.gems=20) or the console (__engine.set('game.gems', 20)).\n268\t      defineSettings({\n269\t        'game.gems': {\n270\t          type: 'integer', default: 10, minimum: 1, maximum: 40, when: 'scene', description: 'How many gems.',\n271\t        },\n272\t        'game.seconds': {\n273\t          type: 'number', default: 30, minimum: 5, maximum: 600, unit: 's', when: 'scene', description: 'The clock.',\n274\t        },\n275\t        'game.speed': {\n276\t          type: 'number', default: 6, minimum: 1, maximum: 20, unit: 'm/s', when: 'scene', description: 'Run speed.',\n277\t        },\n278\t      });\n279\t\n280\t      // Components: data on entities. A component with x and z in metres and a material is drawn by the page.\n281\t      defineComponent('gem', {\n282\t        description: 'A gem to collect.',\n283\t        fields: {\n284\t          x: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'East.' },\n285\t          y: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, unit: 'm', description: 'Up.' },\n286\t          z: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'South.' },\n287\t          yaw: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'rad', description: 'Its spin.' },\n288\t          size: { type: 'number', default: 0.4, unit: 'm', description: 'Its size.' },\n289\t          material: { type: 'string', default: 'material:glow', description: 'How it is drawn.' },\n290\t        },\n291\t      });\n292\t      defineComponent('round', {\n293\t        description: 'The round: gems collected, steps left on the clock, and whether it is playing, won or lost.',\n294\t        fields: {\n295\t          gems: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many gems.' },\n296\t          collected: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many are collected.' },\n297\t          left: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'Steps left on the clock (60 a second).' },\n298\t          state: { type: 'string', default: 'playing', description: \"'playing', 'won' or 'lost'.\" },\n299\t        },\n300\t      });\n301\t\n302\t      const REACH = 0.8; // how close the player's feet come to a gem to collect it, m\n303\t      const START = ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero').position;\n304\t\n305\t      // Scatters the gems over floor tiles at least 3 m from the spawn, drawn from the named random stream 'gems'.\n306\t      function scatterGems(w, count) {\n307\t        const tiles = [];\n308\t        ARENA.tiles.forEach((char, i) => {\n309\t          const [x, z] = [((i % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile, (Math.floor(i / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile];\n310\t          if (char === '.' && Math.hypot(x - START[0], z - START[2]) >= 3) tiles.push([x, z]);\n311\t        });\n312\t        const rng = w.rng('gems');\n313\t        for (let k = 0; k < count && tiles.length > 0; k++) {\n314\t          const [[x, z]] = tiles.splice(rng.int(0, tiles.length - 1), 1);\n315\t          w.spawn({ gem: { x, z, yaw: rng.range(0, Math.PI) } });\n316\t        }\n317\t      }\n318\t\n319\t      // Ends the round: the player stops, and the event says how it ended.\n320\t      function endRound(w, round, state) {\n321\t        round.state = state;\n322\t        for (const e of w.query('actor')) {\n323\t          e.actor.player = false;\n324\t          e.actor.intents = {};\n325\t        }\n326\t        w.emit(state, { collected: round.collected, gems: round.gems });\n327\t      }\n328\t\n329\t      // A system: spins the gems and collects those an actor reaches (a despawn waits for the step's end).\n330\t      function collect(w) {\n331\t        const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n332\t        for (const gem of w.query('gem')) gem.gem.yaw += 2 * w.dt;\n333\t        if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n334\t        const taken = new Set();\n335\t        for (const { actor } of w.query('actor')) {\n336\t          for (const gem of w.query('gem')) {\n337\t            if (taken.has(gem.id) || Math.hypot(gem.gem.x - actor.x, gem.gem.z - actor.z) > REACH) continue;\n338\t            taken.add(gem.id);\n339\t            w.despawn(gem.id);\n340\t            round.collected += 1;\n341\t            w.emit('gem', { id: gem.id, collected: round.collected });\n342\t          }\n343\t        }\n344\t        if (round.collected >= round.gems) endRound(w, round, 'won');\n345\t      }\n346\t\n347\t      // A system: the clock counts whole steps, never summed seconds; at 0 the round is lost.\n348\t      function clock(w) {\n349\t        const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n350\t        if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n351\t        round.left = Math.max(0, round.left - 1);\n352\t        if (round.left === 0) endRound(w, round, 'lost');\n353\t      }\n354\t\n355\t      // The scene: the level, its physics, and what happens when it starts.\n356\t      const play = defineScene('game:play', {\n357\t        description: 'Run round the arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out.',\n358\t        level: 'level:arena',\n359\t        physics: true,\n360\t        setup(w) {\n361\t          addLevelBodies(physicsOf(w), ARENA);\n362\t          const gems = w.settings.get('game.gems');\n363\t          w.spawn({ round: { gems, left: Math.round(w.settings.get('game.seconds') * w.hz) } });\n364\t          scatterGems(w, gems);\n365\t          const character = createCharacter({ speed: w.settings.get('game.speed'), jumpSpeed: 9 });\n366\t          const anim = { build: 'build:chibi', weapon: 'none' }; // a humanoid that animates itself\n367\t          spawnActor(w, { at: [START[0], START[1], START[2]], character, player: true, anim });\n368\t          w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' });\n369\t          w.systems.add('game.clock', clock, { phase: 'rules' });\n370\t        },\n371\t      });\n372\t\n373\t      // ---- The page: draws the run and shows the HUD. It only reads the sim, never changes it. ----\n374\t\n375\t      const search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\n376\t      if (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase'); // the camera follows the player\n377\t      let engine = null;\n378\t      let ended = false;\n379\t\n380\t      // Each drawn frame: the gems, the clock, the keys, and how the round ended.\n381\t      function showRound({ world }) {\n382\t        const entity = world.query('round')[0];\n383\t        if (!engine || !entity) return;\n384\t        const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\n385\t        engine.ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\n386\t        const color = left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '';\n387\t        engine.ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color });\n388\t        engine.ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\n389\t        ended = state !== 'playing';\n390\t        engine.ui.hud('end', ended ? `${state === 'won' ? 'Every gem!' : 'Time!'} Enter: play again` : null, {\n391\t          at: 'top',\n392\t        });\n393\t      }\n394\t\n395\t      engine = await createEngine({\n396\t        canvas: document.querySelector('#view'),\n397\t        scenes: [play.id],\n398\t        levels: { 'level:arena': { text: ARENA_TEXT, file: 'game.html' } },\n399\t        search: `?${search}`,\n400\t        onFrame: showRound,\n401\t      });\n402\t      document.getElementById('msg').hidden = true;\n403\t\n404\t      // Enter after a round: the next round, on the next seed.\n405\t      addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n406\t        if (event.code === 'Enter' && ended) engine.inspector.seed(engine.inspector.seed() + 1);\n407\t      });\n408\t    </script>\n409\t  </body>\n410\t</html>\n411\t```\n412\t<!-- lean:game:end -->\n413\t\n414\t## Looks and filters\n415\t\n416\t<!-- lean:looks:start -->\n417\t**Whole looks**, each with what it draws (its style and sky where it names them, its filters and their scopes):\n418\t\n419\t- `look:16-bit`: The whole scene as 16-bit pixel art with a slightly vivid grade. _filters pixel, grade._\n420\t- `look:anime-action`: An action frame: comic cel bands, bold lines and speed lines. _filters cel, outline, speedlines._\n421\t- `look:arcade-cabinet`: An arcade cabinet: a curved tube's grille, glow and lines. _filters bloom, scanlines, crt, screen._\n422\t- `look:arcade-crt`: Low resolution on a curved tube with scanlines, glow and colour fringes. _filters bloom, scanlines, chroma, screen._\n423\t- `look:cartoon`: Toon shading everywhere, bold ink outlines and vivid colour. _style toon; filters outline, grade._\n424\t- `look:cinematic`: A film: teal and orange, anamorphic streaks, soft corners and widescreen bars. _filters streaks, grade, split, vignette, letterbox._\n425\t- `look:clean`: No filter: the picture as the scene draws it, in its own style. _nothing._\n426\t- `look:comic-book`: Printed dots on the world, inked heroes, punchy colour. _filters outline, stylize (environment), grade (characters+objects)._\n427\t- `look:comic-noir`: A black-and-white world; the characters and objects keep their colour, inked. _filters outline, grade (environment), screen._\n428\t- `look:flat-paper`: Flat colours, thin outlines and a faded grade, like a printed page. _style flat; filters outline, grade, grain._\n429\t- `look:game-boy`: Everything in four greens, pixelated and dithered. _filters pixel, palette._\n430\t- `look:game-boy-world`: The environment in four Game Boy greens; the characters and objects in full colour with ink outlines. _filters palette (environment), outline (characters+objects)._\n431\t- `look:hacker-terminal`: The game as green characters on a curved, scanlined screen. _filters stylize, scanlines, screen._\n432\t- `look:hd-2d`: Pixel-art characters and objects in a soft, glowing 3D world with dark corners. _filters bloom, pixel (characters+objects), grade (environment), screen._\n433\t- `look:hologram-heroes`: Characters and objects flicker like holograms: cool, scanlined and fringed. The world stays clean. _filters bloom, grade (characters+objects), scanlines (characters+objects), grain (characters+objects), chroma (characters+objects)._\n434\t- `look:lamplit-fog`: A foggy night where every lamp glows in the air. _filters air, grade._\n435\t- `look:miniature`: A model railway: tilt-shift blur, crisp and bright colours. _filters tiltshift, sharpen, grade._\n436\t- `look:newspaper`: Black dots on cream paper, everything. _filters stylize._\n437\t- `look:oil-painting`: The whole scene in oil paint: flat strokes, brush streaks, canvas. _filters stylize, grade._\n438\t- `look:painted-world`: A painted backdrop; the characters and objects stay sharp and inked on top of it. _filters outline (characters+objects), stylize (environment)._\n439\t- `look:pixel-heroes`: A clean, sharp world; the characters and objects are outlined pixel art. _filters pixel (characters+objects)._\n440\t- `look:pixel-world`: The environment is pixel art; the characters and objects stay sharp and inked. _filters pixel (environment), outline (characters+objects)._\n441\t- `look:posterized-world`: The environment in a few flat shades; the characters and objects in full colour. _filters palette (environment)._\n442\t- `look:red-coat`: A grey film where only red keeps its colour, grainy and dark at the edges. _filters grade, selective, vignette, grain._\n443\t- `look:riso-print`: A risograph print: ember duotone, plates out of register, newsprint. _filters misregister, paper, duotone._\n444\t- `look:sketchbook`: Pencil hatching and contours on paper. _filters stylize._\n445\t- `look:sketched-world`: A coloured-pencil world; the characters and objects inked on top of it. _filters outline (characters+objects), stylize (environment)._\n446\t- `look:storybook`: A storybook: watercolour on cold-pressed paper with a thin ink line. _filters watercolor, outline, paper._\n447\t- `look:sunbeams`: Dusty air, sunlight streaming through the gaps, a soft glow. _filters air, bloom._\n448\t- `look:vhs-tape`: A home video: worn tape, soft scanlines, noise and dark corners. _filters scanlines, vhs, vignette, grain, chroma._\n449\t\n450\t**One-filter presets**, `look:<group>/<name>`: each turns its one filter on over everything, to draw alone or lay\n451\ton with `combine` (the `shading` group sets the material style instead, and `screen` the screen's own glass):\n452\t\n453\t- `aa`: smooth\n454\t- `air`: clear-air, dusty-sunbeams, lamplight, light-haze, morning-mist, pea-soup, smoky-hall\n455\t- `ao`: corners\n456\t- `bloom`: dreamy, neon, no-glow, soft\n457\t- `cel`: comic\n458\t- `crt`: arcade-tube\n459\t- `dof`: portrait\n460\t- `duotone`: dusk, ember, ocean\n461\t- `flare`: sun\n462\t- `gi`: bounce-light\n463\t- `grade`: bleach-bypass, cool, faded, lifted, neutral, night, noir, sepia, vivid, warm\n464\t- `grain`: clean, film, glitch, vhs\n465\t- `lens`: barrel, fisheye\n466\t- `letterbox`: film, widescreen\n467\t- `misregister`: risograph\n468\t- `outline`: bold, hand-drawn, heavy-ink, ink, none, thin\n469\t- `palette`: 1-bit, 512-colours, amber, cga, full-colour, game-boy, pico-8, posterize\n470\t- `paper`: canvas, kraft, newsprint, watercolour-paper\n471\t- `pixel`: 16-bit, 8-bit, bold, chunky, classic, dithered, fine, giant\n472\t- `scanlines`: arcade, fine, heavy, soft\n473\t- `screen`: crt, flat-screen, low-res, old-tv, soft-vignette\n474\t- `selective`: green, red\n475\t- `shading`: as-authored, cartoon, flat-2d, smooth-lit, toon-3-band\n476\t- `sharpen`: crisp\n477\t- `speedlines`: action\n478\t- `split`: moonlight, teal-orange\n479\t- `ssr`: metal\n480\t- `streaks`: anamorphic\n481\t- `stylize`: colour-ascii, coloured-pencil, comic-print, fine-print, gouache, loose-sketch, newsprint, off, oil-paint, pencil, terminal, thick-impasto, underpainting\n482\t- `tiltshift`: miniature\n483\t- `vhs`: worn-tape\n484\t- `vignette`: heavy, soft\n485\t- `watercolor`: wash, wet\n486\t\n487\t**The filters**, in the order the chain draws them, each `{ scope, amount, … }` in a look's `filters` or in\n488\t`post.filters`, with each field's default and range; colours are `'#rrggbb'`. A filter marked (the whole picture)\n489\ttakes only `'all'` or `'off'`; the rest also take `'characters'`, `'objects'`, `'environment'` or two joined by\n490\t`+`.\n491\t\n492\t- `ao` (the whole picture): Ambient occlusion (GTAO, engine/gfx/post/shading.ts): creases and corners darken; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–2), `radius` 0.6 (0.05–4 m).\n493\t- `gi` (the whole picture): Bounce light (12 taps a pixel): lit surfaces colour their neighbours, with soft occlusion; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–4), `radius` 1.6 (0.1–6 m).\n494\t- `ssr` (the whole picture): Screen-space reflections on metal surfaces (SSRNode), blurred on rough ones; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–1), `distance` 6 (0.5–50 m), `thickness` 0.15 (0.01–2 m).\n495\t- `air` (the whole picture): Hazy air with light shafts where the sun breaks through, and halos round lamps; never scoped. `haze` 0.35 (0–3), `height` 4 (0.2–20 m), `shafts` 1 (0–3), `forward` 0.4 (-0.9–0.95), `halos` 0.3 (0–3).\n496\t- `bloom` (the whole picture): Bright parts glow (three.js's BloomNode), before the composite, never scoped. `strength` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.9 (0–4), `radius` 0.4 (0–1).\n497\t- `aa` (the whole picture): Edge smoothing over the picture before the filters (engine/gfx/post/aa.ts).\n498\t- `lens` (the whole picture): Lens distortion: barrel (above 0) or pincushion (below), zoomed so the corners stay filled. `distortion` 0.12 (-0.5–0.5).\n499\t- `dof` (the whole picture): Depth of field: sharp round the focus, blurred nearer and farther, as a camera lens draws it. `focus` 0 (0–500 m), `range` 8 (0.5–60 m), `blur` 6 (0–24 px).\n500\t- `tiltshift` (the whole picture): Tilt-shift: a sharp horizontal band, the rest blurred, so the scene looks like a miniature. `center` 0.55 (0–1), `width` 0.18 (0.02–0.8), `blur` 8 (0–24 px).\n501\t- `flare` (the whole picture): Lens flare: ghosts of the brightest parts mirrored through the centre of the screen. `intensity` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.8 (0–1), `spacing` 0.25 (0.05–0.6).\n502\t- `streaks` (the whole picture): Anamorphic streaks: bright parts drawn out sideways in a tinted line. `intensity` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.75 (0–1), `length` 48 (4–160 px), `tint` '#5f8dff'.\n503\t- `sharpen`: Sharpen: an unsharp mask that crisps edges and texture. `strength` 0.5 (0–2), `radius` 1 (0.5–4 px).\n504\t- `misregister`: Print misregistration: the red and blue plates offset, as on a cheap print. `offset` 1.2 (0–8 px), `angle` 30 (-180–180 °).\n505\t- `cel`: Comic cel: light in flat bands keeping each colour hue, pushed colour, ink at sharp changes. `bands` 4 (2–8), `punch` 1.3 (0–3), `ink` 0.9 (0–1), `inkWidth` 1.5 (0.5–4 px).\n506\t- `pixel`: Pixel art: whole blocks on the scoped parts and on objects tagged pixel art. `size` 4 (0–32 px), `levels` 0 (0–64), `outline` true, `snap` true, `smooth` false.\n507\t- `palette`: Colour reduction: levels per channel or a fixed palette, with ordered (Bayer) dithering. `palette` 'none' (none|gameboy|pico8|cga|1bit|amber), `levels` 8 (0–64), `dither` 0 (0–1).\n508\t- `watercolor`: Watercolour: colour bleeds, darkens where washes meet, granulates in the paper and wobbles. `wobble` 2.5 (0–8 px), `bleed` 0.3 (0–1), `edges` 0.45 (0–1), `granulation` 0.35 (0–1).\n509\t- `outline`: Outlines where depth jumps, surfaces crease or outline groups meet. `width` 1 (1–4 px), `lines` 0 (0–2160), `darken` 0.35 (0–1), `color` '', `depth` 0.02 (0.001–1), `crease` 0.6 (-1–1), `fade` 0 (0–1), `fadeStart` 25 (0–500 m), `fadeEnd` 120 (1–2000 m), `wobble` 0 (0–4 px), `boil` 0 (0–24).\n510\t- `stylize`: Painterly and print styles: oil paint, CMYK halftone, ASCII and pencil. `style` 'paint' (paint|halftone|ascii|sketch), `size` 4 (1–24 px), `mix` 1 (0–1), `color` 1 (0–1).\n511\t- `paper`: Paper or canvas under the picture: it tints and textures the light areas, ink stays dark. `kind` 'newsprint' (newsprint|coldpress|canvas|kraft), `scale` 1 (0.25–4 ×), `strength` 0.4 (0–1), `color` '#f3ead3'.\n512\t- `grade`: Colour grading. `temperature` 0 (-1–1), `tint` 0 (-1–1), `contrast` 1 (0–3), `brightness` 1 (0–3), `saturation` 1 (0–3), `lift` 0 (-0.3–0.3), `gamma` 1 (0.5–2), `vibrance` 0 (-1–1), `hue` 0 (-180–180 °), `sepia` 0 (0–1), `bleach` 0 (0–1).\n513\t- `split`: Split toning: the shadows toward one colour and the highlights toward another. `shadows` '#2e5f8a', `highlights` '#ffb46b', `balance` 0 (-1–1).\n514\t- `selective`: Selective colour: every colour but one turned grey (a red coat in a grey film). `hue` 0 (0–360 °), `range` 35 (5–120 °).\n515\t- `duotone`: Gradient map: brightness mapped onto a dark, a middle and a light colour (duotone, tritone). `dark` '#1b1442', `mid` '#b04a6f', `light` '#fff1c9'.\n516\t- `scanlines`: Dark lines across the screen, as on a tube. `strength` 0.5 (0–1), `period` 3 (1–16 px)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21849288-0c8f-4994-9d0f-3ff83c647ec0","uuid":"c20265cc-da67-4a9f-8895-681204eb2fbc","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:57.673Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211902__xe334ec/work/_scratch/my-3dge/lean/README.md","content":"# my-3dge, lean\n\nThe my-3dge game engine as a library, the way three.js or Rapier ship: one ES module, nothing to install, build or\ntest. It makes 3D games for the browser: three.js r182 draws on WebGPU, Rapier simulates the physics, a fixed 60 Hz\nsim runs the rules, and HTML/CSS draws the UI. This file is all you need, in a chat with no sandbox too: a game is\none HTML file.\n\n## Start\n\n1. Take `game.html` (in full below): a small game that already plays. You run round an arena and collect the gems\n   before the clock runs out.\n2. Change it into the game you are asked for. Keep its head: it loads the engine, three.js and Rapier from jsDelivr.\n3. Open it in a browser with WebGPU (current Chrome, Edge or Safari; Firefox where it has WebGPU): double-click it, or\n   serve its folder (`npx serve`, `python3 -m http.server`). There is no WebGL fallback.\n\nA bigger game can split its code into module files that each import `my3dge`; serve the folder then, since browsers\nload module files over http, not from a double-clicked page.\n\n## How a game is made\n\n**Two halves.** The _sim_ is the rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them every\nstep, and events. The _page_ calls `createEngine`, which starts the sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into\nintents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame` for the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The\npage reads the sim and never writes it.\n\n**A scene** is a game mode: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings, setup(w), step(w, intents) })`.\n`setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. A page offers its scenes with\n`createEngine({ scenes })`, and `?scene=<id>` picks one.\n\n**The world, `w`,** is what systems get:\n\n- `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields (`number`, `integer`,\n  `string`, `boolean`) with defaults. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an id; `w.query('gem')` returns the\n  entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)` the entity (`.gem`), `w.add(id, 'gem', values)`, `w.remove(id,\n  'gem')` and `w.despawn(id)` do the rest. A component with `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box, which\n  also reads `y` (its bottom), `yaw`, `size` (its side, 0.5 m) and `material` (an id): name a place you only keep\n  otherwise.\n- Systems run in phases, in order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`:\n  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` runs after the physics moved the bodies.\n- `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)`; a page reads\n  `frame.world.events.trace()`.\n- Time is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800).\n- Randomness comes from named streams: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n- Settings are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum, when:\n  'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`, set from the URL (`?game.gems=20`).\n\n**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once a step: `move` (a direction `[x, z]`), buttons held and\npressed, and the game's own keys. A player actor (`spawnActor(w, { player: true, … })`) reads them itself; systems\nget them as their second argument (`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). WASD or the arrows run,\nSpace jumps, Shift dashes.\n\n**The HUD** is the page's: `engine.ui.hud(id, text, { at })` sets a line at one of six places (`top-left`, `top`,\n`top-right`, `bottom-left`, `bottom`, `bottom-right`), and `null` as its text removes it.\n\n**Particles, sound and lights.** The sim says what happened with events; the page turns them into particles and\nsound, so play stays reproducible:\n\n- A sim event bursts every emitter whose `events` names its type, where its payload says (`position`, or `x`, `y`,\n  `z`): `w.emit('hit', { x, y: 1, z })` throws sparks. To burst others on your own event, lay them into an emitter:\n  `defineEmitter('emitter:lit', { events: ['lit'], layers: [{ emitter: 'emitter:fireflies', count: 24 }] })`, then\n  `w.emit('lit', { x, y: 1.2, z })`.\n- Sound plays in the page: `const audio = createAudio();` once (it starts at the player's first key or click), then\n  `audio.play('sfx:coin')`, or in `onFrame` each new event whose payload names one, `w.emit('lit', { sfx:\n  'sfx:powerup', x, z })` in the sim: `for (const e of frame.world.events.trace()) if (e.seq > heard) { heard =\n  e.seq; audio.event(e.type, e.payload); }`.\n- A prop with a light (`prop:lantern`, `prop:lamp`, …) is switchable when spawned with `{ on }` or `{ group }`:\n  `const id = spawnProp(w, { prop: 'prop:lantern', at: [x, 0, z], yaw: 0 }, { on: false })`, then\n  `w.get(id).propLight.on = true` lights it, and `switchGroup(w, group, true)` a group.\n\n**Characters, physics and the rest** are in the API below, area by area, and every built-in id under [Built-in ids\nand the level legend](#built-in-ids-and-the-level-legend): levels as text (`compileLevel`: a tile at column c and\nrow r is at x = (c + 0.5) × tile, z = (r + 0.5) × tile; `level.spawns` are `{ name, position }`, `'hero'` and `'1'`\nto `'9'`) and sites of buildings on a terrain (`defineSite`),\ncharacters (`createCharacter`, Apex Legends' movement with `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`: sprint, slide, mantle,\nclimb; `spawnActor`), physics (`physicsOf(w)` and its `.raycast`, `.overlap`), props, ladders, ziplines, doors,\nwindows and switches, crowds and steering, combat, melee and firearms (`addMelee`, `defineWeapon`), materials and\ntextures as data, looks and post-processing, particle emitters, sound as data, cameras (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=chase`,\nthe shooter's `?cam3=shoulder`), the HUD (`engine.ui.hud`), menus and a store for saves and settings (`createMenu`,\n`createStore`, `createEngine({ store, modal })`) and touch controls.\n\n## Rules that keep play reproducible\n\nThe engine runs the same seed and the same intents to the same state, bit for bit, on every machine. Keep these in\nthe sim (a scene's `setup`, `step` and systems) and replays, `__engine.hash()` and capture and restore keep working:\n\n- No `Date`, timers, `Math.random`, DOM, network or storage in the sim. Randomness from `w.rng`, time in steps.\n- Write the standard `Math` (the sim swaps in portable versions while it steps), but never keep a `Math` function in a\n  variable, and write `Math.pow`, not `**`.\n- Keep the sim's state in components, not in variables beside it; loop over entities in `w.query`'s order.\n- Presentation never writes the sim: the camera, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change nothing a hash sees.\n- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n\n## Make it look different\n\nHow a game looks is data, apart from how it plays: a _look_ is a material style, a chain of filters and a sky. The\nsame game can be a storybook, a film or pixel art with nothing else changed, and no look changes a replay or a hash.\n\n- **Pick a look:** `?post.look=look:storybook` in the URL. To make it the game's own, set it where game.html sets its\n  camera: `if (!search.has('post.look')) search.set('post.look', 'look:storybook');`. Live, from the console:\n  `__engine.set('post.look', 'look:cinematic')`.\n- **Lay looks on and tune them:** `post.combine` lays looks over the one drawn, each `{ look, amount, only, filters }`\n  (`amount` 0 to 1, `only` some of its filters, `filters` changes to them); `post.filters` changes filters field by\n  field; `post.strength` (0 to 1) fades the whole result. In a URL their values are JSON:\n  `?post.combine=[{\"look\":\"look:vignette/soft\",\"amount\":0.6}]&post.filters={\"grade\":{\"scope\":\"all\",\"saturation\":1.3}}`.\n- **Where a filter applies:** its `scope`: `'all'`, `'characters'` (the actors' and crowds' bodies), `'objects'`\n  (props), `'environment'` (the rest: the level, the terrain, stand-in boxes, the sky), two joined by `+`\n  (`'characters+objects'`), or `'off'`. Every filter has an `amount`, 0 (as if it were off) to 1.\n- **Make your own:** `defineLook` before `createEngine`, then pick it like any look:\n\n  ```js\n  defineLook('look:warm-tale', {\n    description: 'A storybook with soft corners and inked heroes.',\n    combine: [{ look: 'look:storybook' }, { look: 'look:vignette/soft', amount: 0.6 }],\n    filters: { outline: { scope: 'characters', width: 2 }, grade: { scope: 'all', temperature: 0.2 } },\n  });\n  ```\n\n- **Read it back:** `__engine.look()` gives the look drawn now: its id, the looks laid on, the strength, and every\n  filter that is on with its scope, amount and numbers; its `filters` pasted into a `defineLook` keeps the picture.\n\nHow they combine: a look's `combine` goes on first, layer by layer, then its own `filters`, each of which replaces a\nlayer's filter of its name whole, its unwritten fields at their defaults (so `look:warm-tale` inks only the\ncharacters, though `look:storybook` inks everything). A layer at an `amount` below 1 fades in a filter the chain lacks\n(its `amount` scaled) and moves one the chain has that far: numbers move, colours mix, and choices (a scope, a kind)\nswitch from 0.5. A filter stays off\nuntil something gives it a scope: `grade: { temperature: 0.2 }` alone does nothing. A layer never turns a filter off,\nnor does `scope: 'off'` in a look's own `filters`: leave a layer's filter out with `only`, or set its scope to `'off'`\nin that layer's `filters`; `post.filters`, applied last, turns one off outright. Only filters are laid on: the style,\nthe sky and the rest come from the look `post.look` names. Changing a filter's numbers, scope or amount, or the looks\nlaid on, builds no shader. Every look, what it draws, and every filter are under [Looks and\nfilters](#looks-and-filters), below game.html.\n\n## Look inside a running game\n\nThe browser console has `__engine`: `__engine.help()` lists it all; `step(n)`, `hash()`, `state('round')`,\n`set('game.gems', 20)`, `seed(7)`, `camera.set('cam=iso')`, `look()`, and `describe('material')`, a kind's fields and\nids. The URL takes the same: `?seed=7&game.gems=20&cam=iso`.\nErrors name their fix, and docs/ERRORS.md in the engine's repository lists every code.\n\n## Versions\n\nThe head's URLs pin three.js and Rapier, and take the engine at `@main`, its latest: jsDelivr serves a new commit\nwithin 12 hours, and a browser may keep the copy it loaded for up to 7 days (jsDelivr's cache header). To freeze a\ngame on one engine, replace `main` with a commit of https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge in its two engine URLs\n(`my3dge.js`, `my3dge.css`).\n\nMore: the engine's repository has the full guide (docs/GAME-GUIDE.md), every export (docs/API.md) and the source. An\nagent that can run commands and wants the engine's checks too can start from the repository's README instead.\nLicense: MIT; `my3dge.js` carries @stdlib functions (Apache-2.0): see LICENSE beside this file.\n\n## game.html\n\n<!-- lean:game:start -->\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no\" />\n    <title>Gem run: a my-3dge game</title>\n    <!--\n      A whole game in one page: run round an arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out. Make your game by\n      changing this file (lean/README.md says how). Open it in a browser with WebGPU: double-click it, or serve its\n      folder. The block below loads the engine, three.js and Rapier from jsDelivr; keep it as it is.\n    -->\n    <!-- lean:head:start -->\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/michaelcrosato/my-3dge@main/lean/my3dge.css\" />\n    <script type=\"importmap\">\n      {\n        \"imports\": {\n          \"my3dge\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/michaelcrosato/my-3dge@main/lean/my3dge.js\",\n          \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.js\",\n          \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.js\",\n          \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.js\",\n          \"three/addons/\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/examples/jsm/\",\n          \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.21.0/dist/rapier.mjs\"\n        }\n      }\n    </script>\n    <!-- lean:head:end -->\n    <style>\n      html,\n      body {\n        margin: 0;\n        height: 100%;\n        overflow: hidden;\n        background: #15171c;\n      }\n      canvas {\n        display: block;\n        width: 100vw;\n        height: 100dvh;\n        outline: none;\n        touch-action: none;\n      }\n      #msg {\n        position: fixed;\n        inset: 0;\n        display: flex;\n        align-items: center;\n        justify-content: center;\n        padding: 24px;\n        font:\n          18px system-ui,\n          sans-serif;\n        color: #c9ccd4;\n        text-align: center;\n        white-space: pre-line;\n        pointer-events: none;\n      }\n    </style>\n    <script>\n      // Until the engine starts, a script that fails shows here in one line, never as a blank page.\n      window.__engine = { ready: false, errors: [] };\n      function failed(message) {\n        if (typeof window.__engine.help === 'function') return; // the engine shows its own errors\n        window.__engine.errors.push({\n          code: 'APP_START',\n          message: message,\n          fix: 'the browser console names the cause',\n        });\n        document.getElementById('msg').textContent = 'The game failed to start: ' + message;\n      }\n      addEventListener('error', (event) => failed(event.message || 'a script failed to load'));\n      addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (event) => failed(String(event.reason?.message ?? event.reason)));\n    </script>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <canvas id=\"view\"></canvas>\n    <div id=\"msg\" role=\"status\">Loading…</div>\n    <script type=\"module\">\n      import {\n        addLevelBodies,\n        compileLevel,\n        createCharacter,\n        createEngine,\n        defineComponent,\n        defineScene,\n        defineSettings,\n        physicsOf,\n        spawnActor,\n      } from 'my3dge';\n\n      // ---- The sim: everything that decides play. It runs 60 steps a second, the same on every machine. ----\n\n      // The arena as text: # wall, . floor, P pillar, w low wall (jump it), @ the player's spawn. 1 m a character.\n      const ARENA_TEXT = `---\ndescription: The arena. 18 × 14 m inside walls, two pillars, two low walls to jump, the player's spawn.\n---\n##################\n#................#\n#..@.............#\n#.......PP.......#\n#.......PP.......#\n#................#\n#....w......w....#\n#....w......w....#\n#................#\n#.......PP.......#\n#.......PP.......#\n#................#\n#................#\n##################\n`;\n      const ARENA = compileLevel(ARENA_TEXT, { file: 'game.html', name: 'arena' });\n\n      // Settings: change them from the URL (?game.gems=20) or the console (__engine.set('game.gems', 20)).\n      defineSettings({\n        'game.gems': {\n          type: 'integer', default: 10, minimum: 1, maximum: 40, when: 'scene', description: 'How many gems.',\n        },\n        'game.seconds': {\n          type: 'number', default: 30, minimum: 5, maximum: 600, unit: 's', when: 'scene', description: 'The clock.',\n        },\n        'game.speed': {\n          type: 'number', default: 6, minimum: 1, maximum: 20, unit: 'm/s', when: 'scene', description: 'Run speed.',\n        },\n      });\n\n      // Components: data on entities. A component with x and z in metres and a material is drawn by the page.\n      defineComponent('gem', {\n        description: 'A gem to collect.',\n        fields: {\n          x: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'East.' },\n          y: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, unit: 'm', description: 'Up.' },\n          z: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'South.' },\n          yaw: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'rad', description: 'Its spin.' },\n          size: { type: 'number', default: 0.4, unit: 'm', description: 'Its size.' },\n          material: { type: 'string', default: 'material:glow', description: 'How it is drawn.' },\n        },\n      });\n      defineComponent('round', {\n        description: 'The round: gems collected, steps left on the clock, and whether it is playing, won or lost.',\n        fields: {\n          gems: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many gems.' },\n          collected: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many are collected.' },\n          left: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'Steps left on the clock (60 a second).' },\n          state: { type: 'string', default: 'playing', description: \"'playing', 'won' or 'lost'.\" },\n        },\n      });\n\n      const REACH = 0.8; // how close the player's feet come to a gem to collect it, m\n      const START = ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero').position;\n\n      // Scatters the gems over floor tiles at least 3 m from the spawn, drawn from the named random stream 'gems'.\n      function scatterGems(w, count) {\n        const tiles = [];\n        ARENA.tiles.forEach((char, i) => {\n          const [x, z] = [((i % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile, (Math.floor(i / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile];\n          if (char === '.' && Math.hypot(x - START[0], z - START[2]) >= 3) tiles.push([x, z]);\n        });\n        const rng = w.rng('gems');\n        for (let k = 0; k < count && tiles.length > 0; k++) {\n          const [[x, z]] = tiles.splice(rng.int(0, tiles.length - 1), 1);\n          w.spawn({ gem: { x, z, yaw: rng.range(0, Math.PI) } });\n        }\n      }\n\n      // Ends the round: the player stops, and the event says how it ended.\n      function endRound(w, round, state) {\n        round.state = state;\n        for (const e of w.query('actor')) {\n          e.actor.player = false;\n          e.actor.intents = {};\n        }\n        w.emit(state, { collected: round.collected, gems: round.gems });\n      }\n\n      // A system: spins the gems and collects those an actor reaches (a despawn waits for the step's end).\n      function collect(w) {\n        const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n        for (const gem of w.query('gem')) gem.gem.yaw += 2 * w.dt;\n        if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n        const taken = new Set();\n        for (const { actor } of w.query('actor')) {\n          for (const gem of w.query('gem')) {\n            if (taken.has(gem.id) || Math.hypot(gem.gem.x - actor.x, gem.gem.z - actor.z) > REACH) continue;\n            taken.add(gem.id);\n            w.despawn(gem.id);\n            round.collected += 1;\n            w.emit('gem', { id: gem.id, collected: round.collected });\n          }\n        }\n        if (round.collected >= round.gems) endRound(w, round, 'won');\n      }\n\n      // A system: the clock counts whole steps, never summed seconds; at 0 the round is lost.\n      function clock(w) {\n        const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n        if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n        round.left = Math.max(0, round.left - 1);\n        if (round.left === 0) endRound(w, round, 'lost');\n      }\n\n      // The scene: the level, its physics, and what happens when it starts.\n      const play = defineScene('game:play', {\n        description: 'Run round the arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out.',\n        level: 'level:arena',\n        physics: true,\n        setup(w) {\n          addLevelBodies(physicsOf(w), ARENA);\n          const gems = w.settings.get('game.gems');\n          w.spawn({ round: { gems, left: Math.round(w.settings.get('game.seconds') * w.hz) } });\n          scatterGems(w, gems);\n          const character = createCharacter({ speed: w.settings.get('game.speed'), jumpSpeed: 9 });\n          const anim = { build: 'build:chibi', weapon: 'none' }; // a humanoid that animates itself\n          spawnActor(w, { at: [START[0], START[1], START[2]], character, player: true, anim });\n          w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' });\n          w.systems.add('game.clock', clock, { phase: 'rules' });\n        },\n      });\n\n      // ---- The page: draws the run and shows the HUD. It only reads the sim, never changes it. ----\n\n      const search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\n      if (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase'); // the camera follows the player\n      let engine = null;\n      let ended = false;\n\n      // Each drawn frame: the gems, the clock, the keys, and how the round ended.\n      function showRound({ world }) {\n        const entity = world.query('round')[0];\n        if (!engine || !entity) return;\n        const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\n        engine.ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\n        const color = left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '';\n        engine.ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color });\n        engine.ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\n        ended = state !== 'playing';\n        engine.ui.hud('end', ended ? `${state === 'won' ? 'Every gem!' : 'Time!'} Enter: play again` : null, {\n          at: 'top',\n        });\n      }\n\n      engine = await createEngine({\n        canvas: document.querySelector('#view'),\n        scenes: [play.id],\n        levels: { 'level:arena': { text: ARENA_TEXT, file: 'game.html' } },\n        search: `?${search}`,\n        onFrame: showRound,\n      });\n      document.getElementById('msg').hidden = true;\n\n      // Enter after a round: the next round, on the next seed.\n      addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n        if (event.code === 'Enter' && ended) engine.inspector.seed(engine.inspector.seed() + 1);\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n<!-- lean:game:end -->\n\n## Looks and filters\n\n<!-- lean:looks:start -->\n**Whole looks**, each with what it draws (its style and sky where it names them, its filters and their scopes):\n\n- `look:16-bit`: The whole scene as 16-bit pixel art with a slightly vivid grade. _filters pixel, grade._\n- `look:anime-action`: An action frame: comic cel bands, bold lines and speed lines. _filters cel, outline, speedlines._\n- `look:arcade-cabinet`: An arcade cabinet: a curved tube's grille, glow and lines. _filters bloom, scanlines, crt, screen._\n- `look:arcade-crt`: Low resolution on a curved tube with scanlines, glow and colour fringes. _filters bloom, scanlines, chroma, screen._\n- `look:cartoon`: Toon shading everywhere, bold ink outlines and vivid colour. _style toon; filters outline, grade._\n- `look:cinematic`: A film: teal and orange, anamorphic streaks, soft corners and widescreen bars. _filters streaks, grade, split, vignette, letterbox._\n- `look:clean`: No filter: the picture as the scene draws it, in its own style. _nothing._\n- `look:comic-book`: Printed dots on the world, inked heroes, punchy colour. _filters outline, stylize (environment), grade (characters+objects)._\n- `look:comic-noir`: A black-and-white world; the characters and objects keep their colour, inked. _filters outline, grade (environment), screen._\n- `look:flat-paper`: Flat colours, thin outlines and a faded grade, like a printed page. _style flat; filters outline, grade, grain._\n- `look:game-boy`: Everything in four greens, pixelated and dithered. _filters pixel, palette._\n- `look:game-boy-world`: The environment in four Game Boy greens; the characters and objects in full colour with ink outlines. _filters palette (environment), outline (characters+objects)._\n- `look:hacker-terminal`: The game as green characters on a curved, scanlined screen. _filters stylize, scanlines, screen._\n- `look:hd-2d`: Pixel-art characters and objects in a soft, glowing 3D world with dark corners. _filters bloom, pixel (characters+objects), grade (environment), screen._\n- `look:hologram-heroes`: Characters and objects flicker like holograms: cool, scanlined and fringed. The world stays clean. _filters bloom, grade (characters+objects), scanlines (characters+objects), grain (characters+objects), chroma (characters+objects)._\n- `look:lamplit-fog`: A foggy night where every lamp glows in the air. _filters air, grade._\n- `look:miniature`: A model railway: tilt-shift blur, crisp and bright colours. _filters tiltshift, sharpen, grade._\n- `look:newspaper`: Black dots on cream paper, everything. _filters stylize._\n- `look:oil-painting`: The whole scene in oil paint: flat strokes, brush streaks, canvas. _filters stylize, grade._\n- `look:painted-world`: A painted backdrop; the characters and objects stay sharp and inked on top of it. _filters outline (characters+objects), stylize (environment)._\n- `look:pixel-heroes`: A clean, sharp world; the characters and objects are outlined pixel art. _filters pixel (characters+objects)._\n- `look:pixel-world`: The environment is pixel art; the characters and objects stay sharp and inked. _filters pixel (environment), outline (characters+objects)._\n- `look:posterized-world`: The environment in a few flat shades; the characters and objects in full colour. _filters palette (environment)._\n- `look:red-coat`: A grey film where only red keeps its colour, grainy and dark at the edges. _filters grade, selective, vignette, grain._\n- `look:riso-print`: A risograph print: ember duotone, plates out of register, newsprint. _filters misregister, paper, duotone._\n- `look:sketchbook`: Pencil hatching and contours on paper. _filters stylize._\n- `look:sketched-world`: A coloured-pencil world; the characters and objects inked on top of it. _filters outline (characters+objects), stylize (environment)._\n- `look:storybook`: A storybook: watercolour on cold-pressed paper with a thin ink line. _filters watercolor, outline, paper._\n- `look:sunbeams`: Dusty air, sunlight streaming through the gaps, a soft glow. _filters air, bloom._\n- `look:vhs-tape`: A home video: worn tape, soft scanlines, noise and dark corners. _filters scanlines, vhs, vignette, grain, chroma._\n\n**One-filter presets**, `look:<group>/<name>`: each turns its one filter on over everything, to draw alone or lay\non with `combine` (the `shading` group sets the material style instead, and `screen` the screen's own glass):\n\n- `aa`: smooth\n- `air`: clear-air, dusty-sunbeams, lamplight, light-haze, morning-mist, pea-soup, smoky-hall\n- `ao`: corners\n- `bloom`: dreamy, neon, no-glow, soft\n- `cel`: comic\n- `crt`: arcade-tube\n- `dof`: portrait\n- `duotone`: dusk, ember, ocean\n- `flare`: sun\n- `gi`: bounce-light\n- `grade`: bleach-bypass, cool, faded, lifted, neutral, night, noir, sepia, vivid, warm\n- `grain`: clean, film, glitch, vhs\n- `lens`: barrel, fisheye\n- `letterbox`: film, widescreen\n- `misregister`: risograph\n- `outline`: bold, hand-drawn, heavy-ink, ink, none, thin\n- `palette`: 1-bit, 512-colours, amber, cga, full-colour, game-boy, pico-8, posterize\n- `paper`: canvas, kraft, newsprint, watercolour-paper\n- `pixel`: 16-bit, 8-bit, bold, chunky, classic, dithered, fine, giant\n- `scanlines`: arcade, fine, heavy, soft\n- `screen`: crt, flat-screen, low-res, old-tv, soft-vignette\n- `selective`: green, red\n- `shading`: as-authored, cartoon, flat-2d, smooth-lit, toon-3-band\n- `sharpen`: crisp\n- `speedlines`: action\n- `split`: moonlight, teal-orange\n- `ssr`: metal\n- `streaks`: anamorphic\n- `stylize`: colour-ascii, coloured-pencil, comic-print, fine-print, gouache, loose-sketch, newsprint, off, oil-paint, pencil, terminal, thick-impasto, underpainting\n- `tiltshift`: miniature\n- `vhs`: worn-tape\n- `vignette`: heavy, soft\n- `watercolor`: wash, wet\n\n**The filters**, in the order the chain draws them, each `{ scope, amount, … }` in a look's `filters` or in\n`post.filters`, with each field's default and range; colours are `'#rrggbb'`. A filter marked (the whole picture)\ntakes only `'all'` or `'off'`; the rest also take `'characters'`, `'objects'`, `'environment'` or two joined by\n`+`.\n\n- `ao` (the whole picture): Ambient occlusion (GTAO, engine/gfx/post/shading.ts): creases and corners darken; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–2), `radius` 0.6 (0.05–4 m).\n- `gi` (the whole picture): Bounce light (12 taps a pixel): lit surfaces colour their neighbours, with soft occlusion; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–4), `radius` 1.6 (0.1–6 m).\n- `ssr` (the whole picture): Screen-space reflections on metal surfaces (SSRNode), blurred on rough ones; never scoped. `strength` 1 (0–1), `distance` 6 (0.5–50 m), `thickness` 0.15 (0.01–2 m).\n- `air` (the whole picture): Hazy air with light shafts where the sun breaks through, and halos round lamps; never scoped. `haze` 0.35 (0–3), `height` 4 (0.2–20 m), `shafts` 1 (0–3), `forward` 0.4 (-0.9–0.95), `halos` 0.3 (0–3).\n- `bloom` (the whole picture): Bright parts glow (three.js's BloomNode), before the composite, never scoped. `strength` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.9 (0–4), `radius` 0.4 (0–1).\n- `aa` (the whole picture): Edge smoothing over the picture before the filters (engine/gfx/post/aa.ts).\n- `lens` (the whole picture): Lens distortion: barrel (above 0) or pincushion (below), zoomed so the corners stay filled. `distortion` 0.12 (-0.5–0.5).\n- `dof` (the whole picture): Depth of field: sharp round the focus, blurred nearer and farther, as a camera lens draws it. `focus` 0 (0–500 m), `range` 8 (0.5–60 m), `blur` 6 (0–24 px).\n- `tiltshift` (the whole picture): Tilt-shift: a sharp horizontal band, the rest blurred, so the scene looks like a miniature. `center` 0.55 (0–1), `width` 0.18 (0.02–0.8), `blur` 8 (0–24 px).\n- `flare` (the whole picture): Lens flare: ghosts of the brightest parts mirrored through the centre of the screen. `intensity` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.8 (0–1), `spacing` 0.25 (0.05–0.6).\n- `streaks` (the whole picture): Anamorphic streaks: bright parts drawn out sideways in a tinted line. `intensity` 0.6 (0–3), `threshold` 0.75 (0–1), `length` 48 (4–160 px), `tint` '#5f8dff'.\n- `sharpen`: Sharpen: an unsharp mask that crisps edges and texture. `strength` 0.5 (0–2), `radius` 1 (0.5–4 px).\n- `misregister`: Print misregistration: the red and blue plates offset, as on a cheap print. `offset` 1.2 (0–8 px), `angle` 30 (-180–180 °).\n- `cel`: Comic cel: light in flat bands keeping each colour hue, pushed colour, ink at sharp changes. `bands` 4 (2–8), `punch` 1.3 (0–3), `ink` 0.9 (0–1), `inkWidth` 1.5 (0.5–4 px).\n- `pixel`: Pixel art: whole blocks on the scoped parts and on objects tagged pixel art. `size` 4 (0–32 px), `levels` 0 (0–64), `outline` true, `snap` true, `smooth` false.\n- `palette`: Colour reduction: levels per channel or a fixed palette, with ordered (Bayer) dithering. `palette` 'none' (none|gameboy|pico8|cga|1bit|amber), `levels` 8 (0–64), `dither` 0 (0–1).\n- `watercolor`: Watercolour: colour bleeds, darkens where washes meet, granulates in the paper and wobbles. `wobble` 2.5 (0–8 px), `bleed` 0.3 (0–1), `edges` 0.45 (0–1), `granulation` 0.35 (0–1).\n- `outline`: Outlines where depth jumps, surfaces crease or outline groups meet. `width` 1 (1–4 px), `lines` 0 (0–2160), `darken` 0.35 (0–1), `color` '', `depth` 0.02 (0.001–1), `crease` 0.6 (-1–1), `fade` 0 (0–1), `fadeStart` 25 (0–500 m), `fadeEnd` 120 (1–2000 m), `wobble` 0 (0–4 px), `boil` 0 (0–24).\n- `stylize`: Painterly and print styles: oil paint, CMYK halftone, ASCII and pencil. `style` 'paint' (paint|halftone|ascii|sketch), `size` 4 (1–24 px), `mix` 1 (0–1), `color` 1 (0–1).\n- `paper`: Paper or canvas under the picture: it tints and textures the light areas, ink stays dark. `kind` 'newsprint' (newsprint|coldpress|canvas|kraft), `scale` 1 (0.25–4 ×), `strength` 0.4 (0–1), `color` '#f3ead3'.\n- `grade`: Colour grading. `temperature` 0 (-1–1), `tint` 0 (-1–1), `contrast` 1 (0–3), `brightness` 1 (0–3), `saturation` 1 (0–3), `lift` 0 (-0.3–0.3), `gamma` 1 (0.5–2), `vibrance` 0 (-1–1), `hue` 0 (-180–180 °), `sepia` 0 (0–1), `bleach` 0 (0–1).\n- `split`: Split toning: the shadows toward one colour and the highlights toward another. `shadows` '#2e5f8a', `highlights` '#ffb46b', `balance` 0 (-1–1).\n- `selective`: Selective colour: every colour but one turned grey (a red coat in a grey film). `hue` 0 (0–360 °), `range` 35 (5–120 °).\n- `duotone`: Gradient map: brightness mapped onto a dark, a middle and a light colour (duotone, tritone). `dark` '#1b1442', `mid` '#b04a6f', `light` '#fff1c9'.\n- `scanlines`: Dark lines across the screen, as on a tube. `strength` 0.5 (0–1), `period` 3 (1–16 px).","numLines":516,"startLine":1,"totalLines":854,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Lgunh3xdo2e48ZAu4wvqRU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9NBERmvN8dDwNTvNJx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XmrdvRZV9zoig6GiJ213Dg","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211902__xe334ec/work/_scratch/ai-hub; grep -n -E \"hub:[a-z-]+\" README.md | head -60; echo ----; grep -rn \"hub:\" scripts/scan.mjs | head -30; echo ----; sed -n 80,230p scripts/hub-check.mjs","description":"Find hub metadata tags and read the rest of hub-check"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22714,"cache_read_input_tokens":123060,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":22714},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21849288-0c8f-4994-9d0f-3ff83c647ec0","uuid":"bc74b73e-52d6-4817-95fb-fb9203b21b6e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:59.157Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9NAyoTLaiae7ifJPfd","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01XmrdvRZV9zoig6GiJ213Dg":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211902__xe334ec/work/_scratch/ai-hub; grep -n -E \"hub:[a-z-]+\" README.md | head -60; echo ----; grep -rn \"hub:\" scripts/scan.mjs | head -30; echo ----; sed -n 80,230p scripts/hub-check.mjs","description":"Find hub metadata tags and read the rest of hub-check"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XmrdvRZV9zoig6GiJ213Dg","type":"tool_result","content":"489:In short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\n592:After the picks, **Random Mode** deals. The hub deals every game that declares `hub:session` and\n623:  the browser, as `localStorage['ai-hub:relay-only']`).\n624:- While no game in the library declares `hub:session`, the reference games\n734:own, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\n741:an override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n757:checks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n758:(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\n----\nscripts/scan.mjs:316:        hub: info.hub,\nscripts/scan.mjs:368:      // hub:* meta from the game's own head, then data/overrides.json on top.\n----\nconst record = args.includes('--record');\n/** Extra flags for the browser, e.g. the ones that give it WebGPU where the machine can (lib/kit/README.md). */\nconst browserArgs = (option('browser-args', '') || '').split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean);\nconst asPath = (g) => (g.startsWith('/') || /^https?:/.test(g) ? g : `/${g}`);\nconst games = args.filter((a) => !a.startsWith('--')).map(asPath);\nif (!games.length) games.push(...EXAMPLES);\n\n/**\n * The player counts to try: as asked, or by the game's mode, within the seats\n * the game takes. Parallel shares nothing, so 1 and 2 say it all.\n */\nfunction countsFor(mode, [least, most]) {\n  const list = asked ?? (mode === 'parallel' ? '1,2' : '2,3,4');\n  return list.split(',').map(Number).filter((n) => n >= 1 && n <= 4 && (asked || (n >= least && n <= most)));\n}\n\n/** The game's session mode (older tags read as today's) and its seats, from its head. */\nasync function headOf(game) {\n  try {\n    const html = await (await fetch(/^https?:/.test(game) ? game : `${base}${game}`)).text();\n    const meta = (name) => html.match(new RegExp(`<meta\\\\s+name=[\"']${name}[\"']\\\\s+content=[\"']([^\"']+)[\"']`, 'i'))?.[1] ?? null;\n    const [least = 1, most = least] = (meta('hub:session-players')?.match(/\\d+/g) ?? []).map(Number);\n    return { mode: modeOf(meta('hub:session-mode')), seats: [least, most] };\n  } catch {\n    return { mode: null, seats: [1, 4] };\n  }\n}\n\nlet chromium;\ntry {\n  ({ chromium } = createRequire(import.meta.url)('playwright'));\n} catch {\n  console.error('hub-check needs Playwright: pnpm add -D playwright && pnpm exec playwright install chromium');\n  process.exit(2);\n}\n\n/**\n * Runs in every frame before its own scripts: it notes what the page asks of\n * the graphics stack, and session-mode pages are watched for dialogs and sockets.\n */\nfunction watch() {\n  // What the page asked of the graphics stack: a WebGPU adapter it never got, and\n  // no WebGL drawing instead, means this browser cannot run it (a title screen may still show).\n  const gfx = (window.__gfx = { askedForWebGPU: false, gotAdapter: false, webgl: false, twoD: false });\n  if (navigator.gpu?.requestAdapter) {\n    const ask = navigator.gpu.requestAdapter.bind(navigator.gpu);\n    Object.defineProperty(navigator.gpu, 'requestAdapter', {\n      configurable: true,\n      writable: true,\n      value: async (...a) => {\n        gfx.askedForWebGPU = true;\n        const adapter = await ask(...a);\n        if (adapter) gfx.gotAdapter = true;\n        return adapter;\n      },\n    });\n  }\n  for (const Canvas of [window.HTMLCanvasElement, window.OffscreenCanvas].filter(Boolean)) {\n    const getContext = Canvas.prototype.getContext;\n    Canvas.prototype.getContext = function (type, ...rest) {\n      const context = getContext.call(this, type, ...rest);\n      if (context && /^(webgl2?|experimental-webgl)$/.test(type)) gfx.webgl = true;\n      if (context && type === '2d') gfx.twoD = true;\n      return context;\n    };\n  }\n\n  if (!/[?&]aihub=/.test(location.search)) return;\n  const seen = (window.__hubViolations = []);\n  for (const name of ['alert', 'confirm', 'prompt', 'open']) {\n    window[name] = () => {\n      seen.push(`${name}() called`);\n      return name === 'confirm' ? false : null;\n    };\n  }\n  for (const name of ['WebSocket', 'RTCPeerConnection', 'EventSource']) {\n    const Original = window[name];\n    if (!Original) continue;\n    window[name] = function (...a) {\n      seen.push(`${name} opened in a session (${String(a[0]).slice(0, 80)})`);\n      return new Original(...a);\n    };\n  }\n  addEventListener('error', (e) => seen.push(`error: ${e.message}`));\n  addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (e) => seen.push(`unhandled rejection: ${e.reason?.message || e.reason}`));\n}\n\nlet server = null;\nif (!base) {\n  server = createServer(staticHandler(ROOT));\n  await new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', resolve));\n  base = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`;\n}\n\nconst browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true, executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH, args: ['--no-sandbox', '--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required', ...browserArgs] });\nconst runs = [];\ntry {\n  for (const game of games) {\n    if (drawsOnly) {\n      const report = await stillWorks(game, null, { bare: true });\n      runs.push({ game, players: 1, scenario: 'draws', pass: report.pass, checks: report.checks });\n      if (!asJson) console.log(`${report.pass ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}  ${game}  draws on its own`);\n      continue;\n    }\n    const client = await clientCheck(game);\n    if (client) {\n      runs.push({ game, players: 0, scenario: 'static', pass: client.pass, checks: [client] });\n      if (!asJson && !client.pass) console.log(`FAIL  ${game}  ✗ ${client.name} — ${client.detail}`);\n    }\n    const { mode, seats } = await headOf(game);\n    const counts = countsFor(mode, seats);\n    // A retrofit is played once, at 2 players with one leaving: nothing is shared, so that says it all.\n    const plan = retrofit && !asked ? [] : counts.map((n) => ({ n, scenario: 'standard' }));\n    const leaving = retrofit && !asked ? 2 : Math.max(2, Math.min(3, Math.max(...counts, 0)));\n    if (asked || leaving <= seats[1]) plan.push({ n: leaving, scenario: 'host-leave' });\n    // A shared world must also run on a copy with no player of its own, as a server would run it.\n    if (!retrofit && mode && !SELF_REPORTED.includes(mode) && (asked || (seats[0] <= 2 && seats[1] >= 2))) plan.push({ n: 2, scenario: 'server' });\n    for (const { n, scenario } of plan) {\n      const context = await browser.newContext({ viewport: { width: 1280, height: 860 } });\n      await context.addInitScript(watch);\n      const page = await context.newPage();\n      const pageErrors = [];\n      const warnings = [];\n      page.on('pageerror', (e) => pageErrors.push(e.message));\n      page.on('console', (m) => m.text().startsWith('[AIHub]') && warnings.push(m.text()));\n      const query = { game, players: n, latency: 150, jitter: 50, loss: 5, auto: 1, scenario, play: 8000 };\n      const url = `${base}/lib/hub/harness.html?${new URLSearchParams(query)}`;\n      await page.goto(url);\n      // The harness times every step itself; this only catches a page that stopped answering.\n      // Four copies of a 3D game on software WebGL can take minutes.\n      const finished = await page.waitForSelector('body[data-complete=\"true\"]', { timeout: PAGE_MS }).then(() => true, () => false);\n      const report = (await page.evaluate(() => window.__hubCheck).catch(() => null)) || { pass: false, checks: [] };\n      if (!finished) {\n        report.pass = false;\n        report.checks.push({ name: `the harness finished within ${PAGE_MS / 1000} s`, pass: false, detail: `stopped after: ${report.checks.at(-1)?.name ?? 'nothing'}` });\n      }\n      const extra = [\n        { name: 'no client warnings (oversized or too many sends, misuse)', pass: !warnings.length, detail: [...new Set(warnings)].slice(0, 4).join('; ') },\n        { name: 'harness page has no errors', pass: !pageErrors.length, detail: pageErrors.join('; ') },\n      ];\n      report.checks.push(...extra);\n      report.pass = report.pass && extra.every((c) => c.pass);\n      runs.push({ game, players: n, scenario, pass: report.pass, checks: report.checks });\n      if (!asJson) {\n        console.log(`${report.pass ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}  ${game}  ${n} players  ${scenario}`);\n        for (const c of report.checks) if (!c.pass) console.log(`        ✗ ${c.name}${c.detail ? ` — ${c.detail}` : ''}`);\n      }\n      await context.close();\n    }\n    if (solo) {\n      const report = await stillWorks(game, original && asPath(original));","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21849288-0c8f-4994-9d0f-3ff83c647ec0","uuid":"8ff924be-b37b-4721-bc4f-8de936c3e4b0","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:59.183Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"489:In short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\n592:After the picks, **Random Mode** deals. The hub deals every game that declares `hub:session` and\n623:  the browser, as `localStorage['ai-hub:relay-only']`).\n624:- While no game in the library declares `hub:session`, the reference games\n734:own, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\n741:an override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n757:checks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n758:(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\n----\nscripts/scan.mjs:316:        hub: info.hub,\nscripts/scan.mjs:368:      // hub:* meta from the game's own head, then data/overrides.json on top.\n----\nconst record = args.includes('--record');\n/** Extra flags for the browser, e.g. the ones that give it WebGPU where the machine can (lib/kit/README.md). */\nconst browserArgs = (option('browser-args', '') || '').split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean);\nconst asPath = (g) => (g.startsWith('/') || /^https?:/.test(g) ? g : `/${g}`);\nconst games = args.filter((a) => !a.startsWith('--')).map(asPath);\nif (!games.length) games.push(...EXAMPLES);\n\n/**\n * The player counts to try: as asked, or by the game's mode, within the seats\n * the game takes. Parallel shares nothing, so 1 and 2 say it all.\n */\nfunction countsFor(mode, [least, most]) {\n  const list = asked ?? (mode === 'parallel' ? '1,2' : '2,3,4');\n  return list.split(',').map(Number).filter((n) => n >= 1 && n <= 4 && (asked || (n >= least && n <= most)));\n}\n\n/** The game's session mode (older tags read as today's) and its seats, from its head. */\nasync function headOf(game) {\n  try {\n    const html = await (await fetch(/^https?:/.test(game) ? game : `${base}${game}`)).text();\n    const meta = (name) => html.match(new RegExp(`<meta\\\\s+name=[\"']${name}[\"']\\\\s+content=[\"']([^\"']+)[\"']`, 'i'))?.[1] ?? null;\n    const [least = 1, most = least] = (meta('hub:session-players')?.match(/\\d+/g) ?? []).map(Number);\n    return { mode: modeOf(meta('hub:session-mode')), seats: [least, most] };\n  } catch {\n    return { mode: null, seats: [1, 4] };\n  }\n}\n\nlet chromium;\ntry {\n  ({ chromium } = createRequire(import.meta.url)('playwright'));\n} catch {\n  console.error('hub-check needs Playwright: pnpm add -D playwright && pnpm exec playwright install chromium');\n  process.exit(2);\n}\n\n/**\n * Runs in every frame before its own scripts: it notes what the page asks of\n * the graphics stack, and session-mode pages are watched for dialogs and sockets.\n */\nfunction watch() {\n  // What the page asked of the graphics stack: a WebGPU adapter it never got, and\n  // no WebGL drawing instead, means this browser cannot run it (a title screen may still show).\n  const gfx = (window.__gfx = { askedForWebGPU: false, gotAdapter: false, webgl: false, twoD: false });\n  if (navigator.gpu?.requestAdapter) {\n    const ask = navigator.gpu.requestAdapter.bind(navigator.gpu);\n    Object.defineProperty(navigator.gpu, 'requestAda