{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VAQCFyXyfZLYyd5mNp4Hwm","type":"tool_result","content":"engine/world/ai.ts\nengine/world/aiRoutes.ts\nengine/world/awareness.ts\nengine/world/footsteps.ts\nengine/world/nav.ts\nengine/world/patterns.ts\nengine/world/projectiles.ts\nengine/world/site.ts\nengine/world/siteNav.ts\nengine/world/siteNavClear.ts\nengine/world/siteNavGraph.ts\nengine/world/siteNavGrids.ts\nengine/world/siteQueries.ts\nengine/world/spatial.ts\nengine/world/steer.ts\nengine/world/telegraphs.ts\nengine/world/terrain.ts\nengine/world/terrainNav.ts\nengine/world/things.ts\nengine/world/tileHeap.ts\nengine/world/traverse.ts\nengine/world/weapons.ts\n\nengine/world:\nai.ts\naiRoutes.ts\nawareness.ts\nfootsteps.ts\nlevel\nnav.ts\npatterns.ts\nprojectiles.ts\nprops\nsite.ts\nsiteNav.ts\nsiteNavClear.ts\nsiteNavGraph.ts\nsiteNavGrids.ts\nsiteQueries.ts\nspatial.ts\nsteer.ts\ntelegraphs.ts\nterrain.ts\nterrainNav.ts\nthings.ts\ntileHeap.ts\ntraverse.ts\nweapons.ts\n\nengine/world/level:\nengine/physics/capture.ts:15: *   gravity and the integration parameters), so `restorePhysics` rebuilds a world that steps on exactly like the\nengine/physics/params.ts:3: * integration parameters, pinned rather than left to the release's defaults, and the world's gravity as a setting.\nengine/physics/params.ts:10: * `physics.gravity` is the gravity bodies fall under, in m/s², straight down (−Y), read before every step: a scene\nengine/physics/params.ts:11: * overrides it (`settings: { 'physics.gravity': 30 }`), `x sim --set` and `__engine.set` change it, and a change is\nengine/physics/params.ts:12: * recorded like any other setting. Characters fall under their own gravity (WP 3.2), as in shardfall, where props at\nengine/physics/params.ts:15: * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/20-sim.js:29, 118-122` (one gravity, `numSolverIterations` 4, a length\nengine/physics/params.ts:20: * PHYSICS_SETTINGS['physics.gravity'].default; // 9.81\nengine/physics/params.ts:46:  'physics.gravity': {\nengine/physics/params.ts:53:      'The gravity bodies fall under, straight down; characters have their own (physics.characterGravity, WP 3.2).',\nengine/physics/binding.ts:3: * (`scenePhysics`, declaring `physics.gravity` on its registry if it lacks it); `attachPhysics` binds it to the sim\nengine/physics/binding.ts:53:/** For a scene that asks for physics: a new physics world, with `physics.gravity` declared on `registry` if it lacks it. */\nengine/physics/binding.ts:56:  const declared = registry.kinds().includes('setting') && registry.has('setting', 'physics.gravity');\n/**\n * @file Scenes, sim-side (PLAN.md §6.9, §9.0, WP 1.5; doctrines: Reproducible, Agent-operable):\n * `defineScene(id, { level?, settings, setup, step })` registers a scene as an entry of the registry kind `scene`;\n * `startScene` makes its world (a settings store with the scene's settings over the defaults and the caller's over\n * those, a world seeded from the seed, the scene's `step` as the system `scene`, then its `setup`). A session\n * (engine/sim/session.ts `createSession`) starts one that records its inputs. The kernel fixture, the box's scenes and\n * `createHeadless` (WP 1.6) all start scenes here, so no scene imports `app/`; engine/sim/replay.ts plays them.\n *\n * A scene is game code: `setup(w)` spawns, adds systems and listeners (inside `w.run`, so under the fdlibm swap);\n * `step(w, intents)` runs every sim step as the system `scene`, first in the phase `intents`, ahead of the systems\n * setup adds there; `actions` are its dev actions, `(w, args) => void` by name (WP 5.6 adds the engine's own).\n * `level` names the level it loads (WP 2.2 compiles it), `terrain` its ground (WP 10.5, engine/world/terrain.ts) and\n * `site` the levels standing on a terrain (WP 13.15, engine/world/site.ts): the view draws them; its setup adds their\n * bodies. `physics: true` gives it a physics world (physicsOf, WP 3.1).\n * `sounds(record, w)` says which sound a traced sim event plays, `{ sfx, vol?, variant? }`, a list of them (a shot and\n * where it strikes, WP 13.22) or null, for `x audio --replay` (WP 9.4): by default the event payload's own\n * (`payloadSound`); a game that maps its events to sounds sets its map here, so Node hears what its pages play (after\n * G9, ADR-0013 amendment 3).\n * Seeds are whole numbers from 0 (`checkSeed`, `SIM_BAD_SEED`).\n *\n * Invariants: a scene's `settings` are checked against the settings schema when it is defined (`CORE_BAD_SPEC`\n * naming each problem), so declare its settings before it; they apply for the run's lifetime, as an override layer\n * (engine/core/settings.ts), and the caller's settings (`--set`, a replay's `settings`) go on top. Each start makes\n * a new settings store and world, so runs share nothing but the registry. A missing scene id is `CORE_NO_ENTRY`.\n *\n * @example\n * import { createRegistry } from '../core/registry';\n * const reg = createRegistry();\n * let made = 0;\n * defineScene('demo', { description: 'Counts its steps.', setup: (w) => w.spawn(), step: () => made++ }, reg);\n# Templates\n\n`node x new <kind> <id>` writes a working entry of a kind from its template here: the module and its unit test,\nformatted, then its checks (types, lint, its test, docs) and the step that comes next. It never overwrites a file.\n`node x new` lists the kinds with what each id looks like; `node x new <kind> --test` writes a kind's sample, checks\nit and removes it, and `node x new --test-all` tests every kind (the full run, `node x ci --local --full`, runs it),\nso no template rots. tools/cmd/new.ts's file comment is the manual (the manifest's keys, the placeholders, the\nchecks); the `add-entry` skill (.claude/skills/add-entry/SKILL.md) is the loop round it.\n\n## The kinds\n\nEach row is a directory here; tools/cmd/new.test.ts fails a kind this table leaves out, or a path its kind refuses.\n\"Seen with\" names a command that shows or checks an entry beyond its own test; `x new` prints the rest of what to do\nafter writing one (its manifest's `next`). A kind marked \"no\" in \"In a game\" makes no entry there and says what a\ngame does instead (its manifest's `games`).\n\n| Kind           | An entry is                                                    | Its path                               | Seen with                                    \n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------\n| `action`       | a multi-beat sequence a body plays (`defineAction`)            | `labs/<lab>/actions/<name>`            | its test (`actionCheck` through `animCheckBod\n| `body`         | a puppet body on the humanoid's bones (`defineBody`)           | `labs/<lab>/bodies/<name>`             | `node x gallery body body:<name>`            \n| `build`        | the humanoid's proportions (`defineBuild`)                     | `labs/<lab>/builds/<name>`             | its test (`animCheck` of its rest pose)      \n| `camera`       | a camera mode of a game's own, `cam3=<name>` (`defineCamera`)  | `labs/<lab>/cameras/<name>`            | `node x shot <page> --cam cam3=<name>`       \n| `course`       | a playground feel course (`defineCourse`)                      | `labs/playground/courses/<name>`       | `node x feel labs/playground --course <name>`\n| `emitter`      | a particle effect as data (`defineEmitter`)                    | `labs/<lab>/emitters/<name>`           | `node x gallery emitter emitter:<name>`      \n| `feature`      | a rendering technique behind its feature and advice            | `engine/gfx/enhanced/<name>`           | `node x perf <lab> --feature feature:<name>` \n| `geometry`     | a shape built from the kit's builders (`defineGeometry`)       | `labs/<lab>/geometry/<name>`           | `node x qa geo --only geometry:<name>`       \n| `interactable` | what a player uses and strikes (`defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`) | `labs/<lab>/interactables/<name>`  | its test (a player's use and punch in a yard)\n| `level`        | a level written as storeys (`defineLevel`)                     | `labs/<lab>/levels/<name>`             | `node x eye <scene>` (a scene that plays it) \n| `look`         | a material style and a filter chain (`defineLook`)             | `labs/<lab>/looks/<name>`              | `node x gallery look look:<name>`            \n| `material`     | three.js's classic parameters as data (`defineMaterial`)       | `labs/<lab>/materials/<name>`          | `node x gallery material material:<name>`    \n| `module`       | a TypeScript module and its unit test                          | `<engine\\|tools\\|labs\\|tests>/<dir>/<name>` | `npm test -- <path>`                    \n| `move`         | a named strike in metres and seconds (`defineMove`)            | `labs/<lab>/moves/<name>`              | `node x gallery move move:<name>`            \n| `movement`     | how a character moves (`defineMovement`)                       | `labs/<lab>/movement/<name>`           | `node x sim <scene> --feel`                  \n| `prop`         | a thing that stands in a level (`defineProp`)                  | `labs/<lab>/props/<name>`              | `node x gallery prop prop:<name>`, `node x qa\n| `rig`          | a procedural creature of engine/anim/proc's blocks              | `labs/<lab>/rigs/<name>`               | its test (`rigCheck`, and a walk)           \n| `scene`        | a box scene and its replay test (`defineScene`)                | `labs/box/scenes/<name>`               | `node x sim`, `node x replay`, `node x shot l\n| `sfx`          | a sound effect for the DSP core (`defineSfx`)                  | `labs/<lab>/sounds/<name>`             | `node x audio sfx:<name>`                    \n| `song`         | a tune in tracker notation (`defineSong`)                      | `labs/<lab>/sounds/<name>`             | `node x audio song:<name>`                   \n| `telegraph`    | an attack's warning shape on the floor (`defineTelegraph`)     | `labs/<lab>/telegraphs/<name>`         | its test (a hit inside, a near miss past it) \n| `texture`      | a seeded, periodic texture generator (`defineTexture`)         | `labs/<lab>/textures/<name>`           | `node x qa tex --only texture:<name>`        \n| `thing`        | what can be hit, as data (`defineThing`)                       | `labs/<lab>/things/<name>`             | its test (struck down, standing again)       \n| `weapon`       | a firearm and the prop it is drawn as (`defineWeapon`)         | `labs/<lab>/weapons/<name>`            | `node x gallery prop prop:<name>`            \n\n`<lab>` is the lab that shows it (`box`, `playground`…; `game` in a game). The gallery (`node x gallery`, PLAN.md WP\n7.7) films what its page registers: its page (tests/pages/gallery.ts) imports every lab's `scenes/`, `looks/` and\n`bodies/`, so an emitter, a prop or a weapon is filmed once a scene imports its module (`--page` films a lab's own\npage instead).\n\n## Adding a kind\n\nA kind gets its template when an agent writes the third entry of it by hand (PLAN.md §8.13); the work package that\nwrites that entry adds the template, and its row here.\n\n1. `tools/templates/<kind>/`: `template.json` and the template files. Its keys: `describe` (one sentence, which\n   `node x new` lists), `id` (what an id looks like, with an example), `pattern` (a regular expression every id\n   matches; let it take `tools/templates/<kind>/<name>`, where the sample is written), `sample`\n   (`tools/templates/<kind>/sample<Kind>`), `files` (template file → target: `{{id}}.ts`, `{{id}}.test.ts`), `checks`\n   (`types`, `lint`, `test`, `docs`), and when they help, `next` (what to do after writing one, which `x new` prints)\n   and `games` (for a kind a game makes none of, what it does instead).\n2. Write it from a real entry of the kind: the file comment's first sentence says \"replace this 